<div class="page">
    <h2 class="section-heading">3.0 CALIBRATION / SET POINT PROCEDURE</h2>

    <div class="content-text" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        It is usually easy to recognize the high and low thermal conductivity conditions when the switch is applied to liquid and vapor conditions (high and low level detection). The higher TC is almost always when the sensors are in the liquid phase.
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">A. Remove power from the TX</h3>
    <ol class="content-text" style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
        <li>Make connections to pins 1 and 3 of Pin 1 (nearest the green LED).</li>
        <li>Connect the TX switch to the power supply.</li>
        <li>Use ABB TX service plug connected to Pin 1.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">B. Calibrate from High TC (Thermal Conductivity) process conditions</h3>
    <div class="content-text" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p>Connect the TX switch to the process medium. The TX will be due to the difference in temperature between the Active Sensor and the Reference Sensor.</p>
        <p>Change the process conditions at the TX sensors from Normal to Alarm, allowing time to stabilize (5 minutes) and log these mV values.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">1. Calibrate from High TC (Thermal Conductivity) process conditions as follows:</h3>
    <div class="content-text" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p>JP3 State A - Green LED, output relay <span data-field="energized_state_3_1">{{ energized_state_3_1 }}</span> at NORMAL condition (High Thermal Conductivity (low differential temperature) conditions at the sensors); high level (above sensor), flow above setpoint (see table page 6)</p>
        <p>Notes:</p>
        <ol class="content-text" style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
            <li>If the TX switch is operated in <span data-field="state_3_1_state">{{ state_3_1_state }}</span>, the TX switch should be adjusted (calibrated) in the NORMAL condition; Highest Thermal Conductivity conditions on the sensors (wet sensor, high flow); Green LED and output relay will be energized during and after calibration.</li>
            <li>CW rotation of R18 turns LED ON.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">Calibration Adjustments</h3>
    <div class="content-text" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p>Provide the NORMAL condition (High Thermal Conductivity process condition (high level (above sensor), high flow)) at the sensor. Green LED and output relay will be energized after calibration.</p>
        <p>Allow 5 minutes (minimum time) for switch temperature to stabilize.</p>
        <p>Adjust R18 (see drawing page 5) as follows:</p>
        <ol class="content-text" style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 10px;">
            <li>If LED is ON - slowly turn R18 clockwise (CW) until the green LED turns OFF.</li>
            <li>If LED is OFF - slowly turn R18 counter-clockwise (CCW) until the green LED turns ON.</li>
            <li>Turn R18 CW as follows for stable operation (no false alarms) (LED will be OFF):</li>
            <li>Air - 1 full turn</li>
            <li>Organics/Hydrocarbons - 2 full turns</li>
            <li>Water - 3 full turns</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <h3 class="sub-heading">3.3 Trimming the TX settings for optimum alarm entry/return to normal time balance if required:</h3>
    <div class="content-text" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p>When the R18 is adjusted such that the LED just turns off, (3.a/b above), the TX 'alarm/recovery (return to normal)' window is positioned nearest to the process's energized LED Normal (high TC, Low mV) value. This provides the shortest possible time to recognize the energized alarm condition, but the longest possible time to enter the de-energized (alarm) state.</p>
        <p>Step 4 is an attempt to provide closer equal response times (time to enter alarm vs. time to recognize normal conditions) and exit the alarm condition) as well as stability from false alarms. Note that adjusting R18 affects both alarm entry time (time for the TX switch to recognize an alarm condition at the sensors) as well as recovery time (time for the TX switch to recognize that the conditions have returned to normal) by positioning the 'alarm/recovery' window nearer the alarm or normal process condition mV values.</p>
        <p>Note: It is important to allow 5+ minutes of stabilization in any new process condition to obtain repeatable time values.</p>
        <p>1. If the alarm entry time needs to be made faster AND the alarm recovery time (time to return to normal conditions) needs to be made longer, turn R18 CW in ¼ - ½ turn increments. Test the switch response after each adjustment until an acceptable balance in alarm entry time vs. recovery time (return to normal conditions) is established empirically.</p>
    </div>

